    {
      "id": "NAOMI",
      "label": "Sega NAOMI",
      "when": [
        "naomi"
      ],
      "input": {
        "name": "Arcade Panel",
        "_comment": "The JVS wire, per player: what Flycast's I/O board reads out of mapleInputState and hands the game as its panel. Up to four players, because the board serves four; a two-player cabinet ignores the rest. Button 1..8 are the JVS buttons in order (Flycast's BTN0..BTN7 - a game names them itself: PUNCH, KICK, SHOT), Coin is the chute, Service and Test are the cabinet's own switches (Test opens the bios menu; a movie that sets up the machine there records it like anything else). Reload is the light gun's off-screen shot. The analog side: Stick X/Y and Stick 2 X/Y are JVS analog channels 0..3 (a wheel, a flight stick), the triggers are what driving games read as gas and brake, Gun X/Y is an absolute screen position, and Rotary X/Y are the relative encoders a trackball or a paddle sends. What a game actually reads depends on the game; the rest is written and never looked at.",
