  "settings": [
    {
      "name": "machine",
      "display": "Machine",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "dreamcast",
        "naomi",
        "naomi2",
        "atomiswave"
      ],
      "default": "dreamcast",
      "description": "Which of Flycast's machines this project is. 'dreamcast' is the console. 'naomi' is Sega's arcade board built from the same parts - cartridge (M1/M2/M4) and GD-ROM games alike - 'naomi2' the two-PowerVR successor, and 'atomiswave' Sammy's board. The three arcade machines take a MAME rom set (the zip, named as MAME names it, because that name is how the set is recognised) and their bios from the firmware page: naomi.zip, naomi2.zip or awbios.zip. A set for another board is refused with a message saying which board it wants. A machine is the whole project: its inputs, its files and its firmware follow from it, and a movie made on one does not run on another."
    },
    {
      "name": "cpu",
      "display": "CPU",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "jit",
        "interpreter"
      ],
      "default": "interpreter",
      "description": "How the SH4 is executed, and it is a different Dreamcast either way - so it is part of the machine a movie was made on, not a speed knob. jit recompiles the game's code to your processor's own: about four times faster, and it is what upstream Flycast runs on every desktop, so it is the Dreamcast most people have actually seen. interpreter walks the code one instruction at a time, which is slower but simpler to reason about when a game misbehaves. They keep the same clock but not the same timing: one charges cycles per instruction and the other per compiled block, so interrupts land in different places and a movie recorded under one will not replay under the other. interpreter is the default, because the recompiler has a fault of its own: on Prince of Persia - The Sands of Time the SH4 branches into memory that holds no code about seventy seconds from power-on, always on the same frame, and the machine stops. That is not the sandbox (a build with no sandbox at all does exactly the same), not Windows (Linux does it too, to the byte), and not every game - Re-Volt and Street Fighter Zero 3 run thousands of frames under jit without a scratch. One difference from a desktop is worth knowing: this build has no address space to give the recompiler, so it runs on Flycast's software translation, which upstream only takes on hosts that cannot do better. Until it is understood the Dreamcast that always runs is the one to start from."
    },
    {
      "name": "port1",
      "display": "Controller 1 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "gamepad",
        "arcadeStick",
        "twinStick",
        "xl",
        "mouse",
        "lightGun"
      ],
      "default": "gamepad",
      "description": "What is plugged into port 1. 'gamepad' is the retail Dreamcast controller. 'arcadeStick' is the Ascii/Arcade stick - the same buttons with no analog at all. 'twinStick' is the Virtual On pair, which reports the second d-pad (P1 Up2..Right2) and no analog. 'xl' is the PantherDC controller, which reports everything: C, D and Z, both d-pads and a second analog stick. 'mouse' and 'lightGun' are different devices entirely and read their own axes - the mouse relative motion, the gun a position on the screen.\n\nA port's device is part of the machine: a game asks the maple bus what is connected and behaves accordingly, so two projects that differ here are two different machines and do not share movies. Only the controller family carries a memory card; a mouse and a gun have no expansion slots, as on the real thing.",
      "when": [
        "dreamcast"
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "port2",
      "display": "Controller 2 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "gamepad",
        "arcadeStick",
        "twinStick",
        "xl",
        "mouse",
        "lightGun"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into port 2. 'gamepad' is the retail Dreamcast controller. 'arcadeStick' is the Ascii/Arcade stick - the same buttons with no analog at all. 'twinStick' is the Virtual On pair, which reports the second d-pad (P2 Up2..Right2) and no analog. 'xl' is the PantherDC controller, which reports everything: C, D and Z, both d-pads and a second analog stick. 'mouse' and 'lightGun' are different devices entirely and read their own axes - the mouse relative motion, the gun a position on the screen.\n\nA port's device is part of the machine: a game asks the maple bus what is connected and behaves accordingly, so two projects that differ here are two different machines and do not share movies. Only the controller family carries a memory card; a mouse and a gun have no expansion slots, as on the real thing.",
      "when": [
        "dreamcast"
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "port3",
      "display": "Controller 3 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "gamepad",
        "arcadeStick",
        "twinStick",
        "xl",
        "mouse",
        "lightGun"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into port 3. 'gamepad' is the retail Dreamcast controller. 'arcadeStick' is the Ascii/Arcade stick - the same buttons with no analog at all. 'twinStick' is the Virtual On pair, which reports the second d-pad (P3 Up2..Right2) and no analog. 'xl' is the PantherDC controller, which reports everything: C, D and Z, both d-pads and a second analog stick. 'mouse' and 'lightGun' are different devices entirely and read their own axes - the mouse relative motion, the gun a position on the screen.\n\nA port's device is part of the machine: a game asks the maple bus what is connected and behaves accordingly, so two projects that differ here are two different machines and do not share movies. Only the controller family carries a memory card; a mouse and a gun have no expansion slots, as on the real thing.",
      "when": [
        "dreamcast"
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "port4",
      "display": "Controller 4 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "gamepad",
        "arcadeStick",
        "twinStick",
        "xl",
        "mouse",
        "lightGun"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into port 4. 'gamepad' is the retail Dreamcast controller. 'arcadeStick' is the Ascii/Arcade stick - the same buttons with no analog at all. 'twinStick' is the Virtual On pair, which reports the second d-pad (P4 Up2..Right2) and no analog. 'xl' is the PantherDC controller, which reports everything: C, D and Z, both d-pads and a second analog stick. 'mouse' and 'lightGun' are different devices entirely and read their own axes - the mouse relative motion, the gun a position on the screen.\n\nA port's device is part of the machine: a game asks the maple bus what is connected and behaves accordingly, so two projects that differ here are two different machines and do not share movies. Only the controller family carries a memory card; a mouse and a gun have no expansion slots, as on the real thing.",
      "when": [
        "dreamcast"
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "bios",
      "display": "Boot ROM",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "hle",
        "real"
      ],
      "default": "hle",
      "description": "Which bios the machine runs. 'hle' is Flycast's own reimplementation, which boots most discs and needs no files - it is not the machine Sega shipped, and anything that reads the bios (the boot animation, the settings screens) is only right with the real one. 'real' asks the project for dc_boot.bin and dc_flash.bin. The two are DIFFERENT MACHINES and do not share movies.",
      "when": [
        "dreamcast"
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "naomiBios",
      "display": "NAOMI Bios Board",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "naomi",
        "hod2bios",
        "f355bios",
        "f355dlx",
        "airlbios"
      ],
      "default": "naomi",
      "when": [
        "naomi"
      ],
      "description": "Which bios set the cabinet has, which is the MAME set the firmware page asks for. 'naomi' (naomi.zip) is nearly every game. A handful shipped in their own cabinets with their own bios and refuse the ordinary one: The House of the Dead 2 takes 'hod2bios', Ferrari F355 Challenge takes 'f355dlx' (the deluxe three-screen cabinet) or 'f355bios' (the twin), and Airline Pilots takes 'airlbios'. Flycast's romset table knows which a game needs; ask for the wrong one and the load says so."
    },
    {
      "name": "rtc",
      "display": "Power-on Clock",
      "type": "int",
      "default": 0,
      "min": 0,
      "max": 2147483647,
      "description": "What time the machine believes it is when it wakes, counted from the Dreamcast's own epoch (1 January 1950). A Dreamcast has a battery-backed clock and writes the time into RAM at boot, where the bios and games read it - so a machine that took the wall clock would be a different machine every run, and a movie recorded on one would not replay on another. This pins it, and a movie records the number it ran with. It also decides the console's ID, which some games read."
    },
    {
      "name": "region",
      "display": "Region",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "japan",
        "usa",
        "europe"
      ],
      "default": "usa",
      "description": "Which region the machine is. On a Dreamcast the region lives in the machine's flash and a game may check it, refuse to run, or simply pick a language. On the arcade boards it picks which of the bios roms in the set is installed - Japan, USA or Export ('europe' here) - and the game follows the bios: language, some content, and whether it boots at all, since a few sets are one region only. When the set has no bios for the region asked, the core takes what it has and says so in the log."
    },
    {
      "name": "language",
      "display": "System Language",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "japanese",
        "english",
        "german",
        "french",
        "spanish",
        "italian"
      ],
      "default": "english",
      "description": "The language set in the machine's own settings, which games read to pick their text.",
      "when": [
        "dreamcast"
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "broadcast",
      "display": "Television Standard",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "ntsc",
        "pal",
        "palM",
        "palN"
      ],
      "default": "ntsc",
      "description": "Which television the machine is wired for. It changes the frame rate and the number of lines drawn, so it is part of the machine rather than a display preference.",
      "when": [
        "dreamcast"
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "coinage",
      "display": "Coin Setting",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "freePlay",
        "coins"
      ],
      "default": "freePlay",
      "when": [
        "naomi",
        "naomi2",
        "atomiswave"
      ],
      "description": "How the cabinet is set to charge, which Flycast writes into the game's EEPROM as it sets the machine up. 'freePlay' is Flycast's own default and what most people have seen: Start begins a game and the Coin button is never needed. 'coins' is the board as the operator got it: a credit per Coin press, however many the game's own settings ask for. It is part of the machine - a movie made under one does not replay under the other."
    },
    {
      "name": "rotation",
      "display": "Vertical Games",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "cabinet",
        "none"
      ],
      "default": "cabinet",
      "when": [
        "naomi",
        "naomi2",
        "atomiswave"
      ],
      "description": "What to do with a game made for a monitor mounted on its side - the vertical shooters and a few others, which Flycast's romset table marks. 'cabinet' turns the picture the way the cabinet did, so it comes out 480 wide and 640 tall and reads upright. 'none' leaves the machine's own 640x480 frame as it is, sideways. Either way the machine ran the same; only the picture a movie shows differs."
    },
    {
      "name": "renderer",
      "display": "Renderer",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "software",
        "opengl",
        "opengl-hw"
      ],
      "default": "software",
      "description": "Which renderer draws the picture. 'software' is skmp's reference rasteriser and the default: it runs entirely inside the sandbox on the CPU, so it is deterministic - the picture is the machine's, not a driver's. It is several times faster than the softpipe below it, and about half the speed of the GPU: Re-Volt, 1500 frames, drawing every frame, is 28s on a Ryzen against 15s on a GTX 1060. 'opengl' is Flycast's own renderer - the one upstream tests and most games are known to look right through - drawing through a Mesa softpipe compiled into the core; also inside the sandbox, also deterministic, and a movie made under either of these two replays under the other. 'opengl-hw' is that same Flycast renderer with its calls leaving the sandbox to your machine's GPU. It is far faster than the softpipe, and it is NOT deterministic: the picture comes from a driver outside the sandbox and the savestate, and differs between machines. A movie recorded on it records that a GPU drew. It also needs a Chimera built with the bridge; without one this draws with the softpipe instead."
    },
    {
      "name": "internalResolution",
      "display": "Internal Resolution",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "1x",
        "2x",
        "3x"
      ],
      "default": "1x",
      "description": "How big a frame the renderer draws before anyone looks at it. A Dreamcast's is 640x480; '2x' and '3x' draw the same frame at 1280x960 and 1920x1440, so edges that were a staircase at 640 come out smooth and an encode has real detail to keep rather than a blur to stretch. It costs drawing time and nothing else. ONLY the OpenGL renderers can do it - the reference rasteriser draws the machine's own 640x480 and ignores this - so it does nothing at all unless Renderer is 'opengl' or 'opengl-hw'.\n\nIt is not part of the machine. Flycast upscales a frame on its way to a screen and leaves it at the machine's own size the moment the frame is something the game will read: a render-to-texture pass that gets copied back into video memory is drawn at 640x480 however this is set, and the dozen or so games Flycast knows read their own picture (Silent Scope, Cosmic Smash, Densha de Go! 2 and the rest) turn that copy on for themselves, so they draw at 1x and are right to. This repository's gate holds the claim: the same program at 1x and at 3x leaves System RAM, video memory, sound memory and the flash byte for byte identical, and only the picture differs."
    },
    {
      "name": "textureFiltering",
      "display": "Texture Filtering",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "machine",
        "nearest",
        "linear"
      ],
      "default": "machine",
      "description": "How a texture is sampled when it does not land one texel to one pixel. 'machine' is what the game asked the PVR for, texture by texture, and is the picture the Dreamcast drew. 'nearest' takes the nearest texel always: pixel art and text stay crisp instead of going soft, which is usually what somebody encoding a video wants, and a texture stretched large goes blocky. 'linear' blends always, which smooths a game that asked for nearest.\n\nOnly the OpenGL renderers have this knob; the reference rasteriser samples the way the PVR does and ignores it. It is a sampler setting and reaches nothing the machine can read - with one exception worth knowing: for the handful of games whose render-to-texture passes are copied back into video memory (Flycast turns that on for them by name), the pixels this changes are pixels the game then reads, so on those games it is part of the machine and not just the picture."
    }
  ],
